WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.419 --> 00:00:07.400
So, how do you build one? Well that starts with the construction event that I briefly mentioned earlier

2
00:00:08.039 --> 00:00:11.910
So what is the construction event again just to just to cover it?

3
00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:18.750
Well, it's an event that we use to spawn rig elements during the preparation phase of the control rig graph

4
00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:21.429
And what does this mean? So

5
00:00:21.989 --> 00:00:27.089
this means that we use this event before the forwards or backwards solve logic to

6
00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.570
spawn any of our rig elements like controls or bones or nulls and

7
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:36.390
It's an event that only runs once at the start of the control rig logic

8
00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:39.520
Now it doesn't just spawn the rig elements

9
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:46.399
but we can also use it to set the initial positions of any of the rig elements that we're spawning or creating and

10
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.039
initialize variables update the hierarchy and

11
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.079
Importantly import the skeleton of the mesh that we're assigning this control rig to

12
00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:01.950
Because as I said before if you were to make any changes to your skeleton the fact that you're

13
00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:07.150
importing the skeleton on the construction event will pick up any of those changes that have been made and

14
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.719
This is essentially the main tool that allows you to procedurally build a control rig

15
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:16.099
And without this without the construction event, none of it would be possible

16
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:22.400
Taking a look at some of the common nodes and functions that you'd use at the construction event here

17
00:01:22.599 --> 00:01:25.280
We have the spawn transform control node

18
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.120
Now this allows you to

19
00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.920
spawn a control and set its position and

20
00:01:30.319 --> 00:01:34.420
Determine what the control actually looks like so the scale of it the shape of it that kind of thing

21
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:41.099
So you could use this to spawn several different controls all at once with all of the same settings

22
00:01:41.099 --> 00:01:46.060
If you know what position that controls in and how the control looks so you're not having to

23
00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:48.659
manually recreate each one and

24
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.379
multiple times

25
00:01:51.379 --> 00:01:57.939
There's also the control stack at item function, which is a function that's pre-built into the engine that is

26
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:02.540
comprised of other procedural rigging nodes like the spawn node

27
00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:05.859
metadata and other string operator nodes and

28
00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:12.900
Its function is similar to that of the spawn transform control node, but with a bit more functionality

29
00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:18.879
So it allows you to add nulls above and below the control that you're spawning

30
00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.580
To add the extra level of control within the hierarchy if you need that

31
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.500
You can also add secondary controls as children of the control that you're spawning

32
00:02:28.539 --> 00:02:33.500
And it's a little easier to do things like rename your controls with the the built-in naming options

33
00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.180
Tags are also a new concept with procedural rigging

34
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:44.379
So they allow you to tag parts of the rig with custom names to access in the rig logic and make updates to many

35
00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:51.539
Parts at once so you could tag all of the bones with the name bone or all of the controls with the name control

36
00:02:51.539 --> 00:02:53.219
for example and

37
00:02:53.219 --> 00:02:57.919
Effect all of those things at once by just finding the items with that tag

38
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.490
Now it's a little bit like setting a variable to access in other parts of the rig logic later

39
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.129
You also have the ability to set metadata on elements within the rig

40
00:03:10.409 --> 00:03:14.250
So storing data on things like controls or bones or nulls

41
00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:21.180
Now this is similar to tagging but think of it more like a big container that can hold multiple data types

42
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:23.500
Unlike just storing a name with the tag

43
00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:30.900
So the value of the metadata that you're setting includes the name as well as the type of element for that particular part of the rig

44
00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.960
so you can just do a bit more with the information and

45
00:03:34.599 --> 00:03:39.759
it's intended to pass data from the construction event to the forwards or backwards solve logic and

46
00:03:40.259 --> 00:03:46.979
There's also the concat or concatenate node and this is useful for adding a suffix to the name of things and

47
00:03:47.259 --> 00:03:49.259
We'll be using this to name our controls

48
00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:55.560
To help us differentiate the names of our controls and our bones as well as build the hierarchy of our rig

49
00:03:56.060 --> 00:04:00.129
We're going to be building a simple FK rig for our character

50
00:04:00.289 --> 00:04:05.430
That you'll be able to use and set up for any of your own skeletal meshes in your own projects

51
00:04:05.969 --> 00:04:09.819
Now, let's take a look at some of the nodes that we're going to be using for this setup

52
00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:15.719
Now some of them you may have used before when you're creating your control rigs

53
00:04:15.919 --> 00:04:20.100
But some of them are specifically intended to be used with the construction event

54
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.670
So it's unlikely you've come into contact with them before but the more of the the more traditional modes and functions

55
00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:32.129
We're going to be using the item name search node now that allows us to essentially

56
00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:38.189
Search for all of the elements within the rig that contain a particular name or even partial name

57
00:04:38.189 --> 00:04:44.339
And this will allow us to grab lots of information at once to use to generate some controls

58
00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:48.779
Now we'll also use the get transform node

59
00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.800
which just allows us to get the transform of a particular element of the rig and

60
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.160
We'll use this information to set the transforms of our controls

61
00:04:58.879 --> 00:05:05.490
Then we'll use the get parent node and this just allows us to search up the chain and find the parent of a particular

62
00:05:05.569 --> 00:05:11.000
Item which we'll use to set up our hierarchy and then the concat node

63
00:05:11.439 --> 00:05:19.490
Which is just going to help us name our controls and a couple of functions that we'll be using are the FK chain function

64
00:05:19.689 --> 00:05:25.970
which is going to connect our controls and our bones together so the controls can drive the movement of the bones and

65
00:05:27.279 --> 00:05:29.639
the colorize controls function

66
00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:36.399
Which allows us to essentially automatically color our controls based on their position

67
00:05:37.459 --> 00:05:43.699
Now some nodes that you may not recognize or at least may not have used before if you haven't worked with procedural rigging yet

68
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:46.860
Are the import skeleton node now

69
00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:52.620
This is exactly what what you think it would be so it imports the skeleton into the control rig hierarchy

70
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:58.740
And it's great for picking up any changes that might have happened to the skeleton like you've added or remove bones for example

71
00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.579
Or it's great for importing a different skeleton when you're using this rig on a different character

72
00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:11.399
We'll also use the spawn control node to spawn all of our controls and set their initial positions and

73
00:06:12.149 --> 00:06:18.790
We'll use the add tag and find items with tag nodes for our bones and our controls

74
00:06:18.790 --> 00:06:26.810
So we'll tag our bones or tag our controls and we use the information in the FK chain function later on and then lastly

75
00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:30.389
We'll take a look at the control stack at item function

76
00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:35.569
just so we can see a different way of spawning the controls and see some benefits of the

77
00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:39.149
Well of this function versus the spawn control node
